#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179602 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179740 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "6f30993e-b5c7-5d8a-9b27-2aed2c9223e1" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Sign of a Circulation From a Diagram"}] :question/id #uuid "6c8b136c-7944-599f-9399-19641212fcbe" :question/math-academy-id "e-9537" :question/problem "![](images/f7/f7e61f72ed7488fd490f42ff519316ed2e87d3c997d64e2cc6b7af36f951dbb5.png)\n\nConsider the circle $S$ and vector field $\\mathbf F,$ shown above. Let $C$ be any path where $S$ is traversed once in the counterclockwise direction. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $\\displaystyle\\oint_C\\mathbf F\\cdot \\textrm d \\mathbf r =  0$\n\n- $\\displaystyle\\oint_C\\mathbf F\\cdot \\textrm d \\mathbf r > 0$\n\n- $\\displaystyle\\oint_{-C}\\mathbf F\\cdot \\textrm d \\mathbf r > 0$" :question/worked-solution "The circulation $\\Gamma$ measures the total work done by a force $\\mathbf F$ in moving a particle along a *closed* curve $C.$\nIt is given by the formula\n\n$$\n\\Gamma = \\oint\\limits_C \\mathbf F \\cdot \\textrm{d} \\mathbf r\n= \\int_a^b \\mathbf F(r(t)) \\cdot \\mathbf r'(t) \\; \\textrm{d}t.\n$$\n\nWe now recall the following:\n\n- If the force works *with* the motion of the particle (i.e., the force helps to push the particle along the path), then $\\displaystyle\\oint_C\\mathbf F\\cdot \\textrm d \\mathbf r > 0.$\n\n- If the force works *against* the motion of the particle (i.e., the force impedes the motion of the particle as it moves along the path), then $\\displaystyle\\oint_C\\mathbf F\\cdot \\textrm d \\mathbf r <                               0.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each statement.\n\n- Statement I is true, while statement II is false. Since the force $\\mathbf F$ acts perpendicular to the motion of the particle as it moves around $C,$ it neither helps nor hinders its motion. \n\n![](images/06/063836a4780ecb4be6e8c6149a422e3c8e9f2770304e659a8116691b91e3d7f1.png)\n\nSo, $\\displaystyle\\oint_C\\mathbf F\\cdot \\textrm d \\mathbf r = 0.$\n\n- Statement III is false. If we reverse the orientation of $C,$ then the situation with regards to the work done by $\\mathbf F$ remains unchanged.\n\n![](images/70/704915d2852e1654aa31d17bd2dfb07a3022fe3d8b597f37bf98bd5af2de1d1b.png)\n\nSo, $\\displaystyle\\oint_{-C}\\mathbf F\\cdot \\textrm d \\mathbf r = 0.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179603 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179741 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "976e05d4-59a8-5a93-be2f-b22357f388b9" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Circulation"}] :question/id #uuid "16605c7c-c238-51ef-84e6-d5b5d58e1539" :question/math-academy-id "e-9539" :question/problem "Calculate the circulation $\\Gamma$ of the vector field $\\mathbf F(x,y) = -y\\,\\mathbf{i} +x\\,\\mathbf{j}$ along the circle $C$ with equation $x^2 + y^2 = 1,$ oriented counterclockwise." :question/worked-solution "The closed curve $C$ is the unit circle. It can be parameterized in the counterclockwise direction as\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r}(t) = \\cos{t}\\,\\mathbf{i} + \\sin{t}\\,\\mathbf{j},\n\\qquad 0 \\leq t \\lt 2\\pi.\n$$\n\nThe circulation $\\Gamma$ measures the total work done by a force $\\mathbf F$ in moving a particle along a *closed* curve $C.$\nIt is given by the formula\n\n$$\n\\Gamma = \\oint\\limits_C \\mathbf F \\cdot \\textrm{d} \\mathbf r\n= \\int_a^b \\mathbf F(r(t)) \\cdot \\mathbf r'(t) \\; \\textrm{d}t.\n$$\n\nAlong the curve $C,$ we have\n\n$$\nx = \\cos t, \\qquad\ny = \\sin t.\n$$\n\nSo,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(t))=-y\\,i+x\\,j=-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing $\\mathbf r'(t),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\,j \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) & =(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j)\\cdot (-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j) \\\\  & ={sin}^{2}⁡t+{cos}^{2}⁡t \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Γ={∮}_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179605 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179743 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "1da220a9-2afc-5b63-a423-a4d2b3de9567" :knowledge-point/title "Circulation in Conservative Vector Fields"}] :question/id #uuid "1e15ae93-0a63-506a-a0ae-b15e035f0494" :question/math-academy-id "e-9543" :question/problem "Consider the vector field $\\mathbf F(x,y) =( e^y+x)\\,\\mathbf i +(xe^y+y^2)\\,\\mathbf j.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $\\mathbf F$ is conservative\n\n- $\\displaystyle\\int\\limits_C\\mathbf F \\cdot \\textrm d\\mathbf r = 0$ for any piecewise-smooth path $C\\in\\mathbb R^2$\n\n- $\\displaystyle\\oint\\limits_C\\mathbf F \\cdot \\textrm d\\mathbf r =0$ for any closed path $C\\in\\mathbb R^2$" :question/worked-solution "If $\\mathbf F$ is a conservative vector field on $\\mathbb R^2,$ then the circulation $\\Gamma = 0$ for any *closed* path $C\\in\\mathbb R^2,$ that is \n\n$$\n\\Gamma = \\oint\\limits_C \\mathbf F \\cdot \\textrm{d} \\mathbf r = 0.\n$$\n\nWe notice that $\\mathbf F = P\\,\\mathbf i + Q\\,\\mathbf j$ is a vector field with domain $D = \\mathbb R^2.$ Computing the partial derivatives, we have\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial P}{\\partial y} = \\dfrac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x} =  e^y.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each statement.\n\n- Statement I is true. The vector field $\\mathbf F$ is conservative since the partial derivatives are equal and continuous everywhere on $\\mathbb R^2.$\n\n- Statement II is false, while statement III is true. The result $\\Gamma = 0$ applies to closed curves only.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180022 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179740 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "6f30993e-b5c7-5d8a-9b27-2aed2c9223e1" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Sign of a Circulation From a Diagram"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "152a2be2-a7fd-5859-a279-3e7945b3da34" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415284} {:answer/id #uuid "645f68e5-b7b0-5de1-8cbc-084d6b2e348a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415292} {:answer/id #uuid "80ef587d-5285-53dc-bc6a-3a360ac67985" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415294} {:answer/id #uuid "9ab16a14-050a-5632-a9a7-2ca2c47d8777" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415295} {:answer/id #uuid "ea5f9cf8-07bb-56bb-8acb-5e6dac6eb062" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415303}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "80ef587d-5285-53dc-bc6a-3a360ac67985" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415294} :answer-field/id #uuid "fb1d5ecc-a424-5137-bd29-d836fb937e8e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415311}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "1d320327-1d15-55df-b5dd-771fb0fd1831" :question/math-academy-id "q-125394" :question/problem "![](images/27/270e72ebedac3741e1b7086021b16dc0975ae9852d402673c8044a67f8f774fb.png)\n\nConsider the ellipse $E$ and the vector field $F,$ shown above. Let $C$ be any path where $E$ is traversed once in the **clockwise** direction. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- ${∮}_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r<0$\n- ${∮}_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r>0$\n- ${∮}_{-C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r>0$" :question/worked-solution "The circulation $Γ$ measures the total work done by a force $F$ in moving a particle along a *closed* curve $C.$\nIt is given by the formula\n\n$$\nΓ={∮}_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r={\\int }_{a}^{b}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nWe now recall the following:\n\n- If the force works *with* the motion of the particle (i.e., the force helps to push the particle along the path), then ${∮}_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r>0.$\n\n- If the force works *against* the motion of the particle (i.e., the force impedes the motion of the particle as it moves along the path), then ${∮}_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r<0.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each statement.\n\n- Statement I is false, while statement II is true. It's clear from the picture below that $F$ assists the motion of the particle as it moves clockwise along $C.$ \n\n![](images/db/dbb6dbdfabccf7a94c67781210238c647e6f3a2e428cb79c4debdbff5e124720.png)\n\nSo, $Γ={∮}_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r>0.$\n\n- Statement III is false. It's clear from the picture below that $F$ works against the motion of the particle as it moves counterclockwise along $C.$\n\n![](images/1c/1c5aa77b0a89e1fcb0782c9d969d3a81bbec47b004f07bd3a53ccd10e623ba6e.png)\n\nSo, $Γ={∮}_{-C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r<0.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186415313 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179741 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "976e05d4-59a8-5a93-be2f-b22357f388b9" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Circulation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1b3484a3-4e24-56fb-b7a3-9ddea884c66d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-16\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415285} {:answer/id #uuid "5f428d96-a179-52b0-a209-2ee6f08dffb0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "16\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415291} {:answer/id #uuid "d9418009-409d-5c05-bc92-ce23127789ee" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415299} {:answer/id #uuid "e65b56f6-b686-5dc3-9782-652e4c386b4a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415302} {:answer/id #uuid "eb8f2775-c0fe-5dbb-b5a2-d9f8ae009d49" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-8\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415304}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "eb8f2775-c0fe-5dbb-b5a2-d9f8ae009d49" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-8\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415304} :answer-field/id #uuid "8daab46c-8511-5579-956f-63a995a70f85" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415310}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "4ec2acb3-1493-5600-bce3-9a9694ba6ec0" :question/math-academy-id "q-125470" :question/problem "Calculate the circulation $Γ$ of the vector field $F(x,y)=y\\,i-x\\,j$ along the circle $C$ with equation ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=4$ oriented counterclockwise." :question/worked-solution "The closed curve $C$ is the circle of radius $2$ centered at the origin. It can be parameterized in the counterclockwise direction as\n\n$$\nr(t)=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,j,\\,0\\le t\\le 2\\pi .\n$$\n\nThe circulation $Γ$ measures the total work done by a force $F$ in moving a particle along a *closed* curve $C.$\nIt is given by the formula\n\n$$\nΓ={∮}_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r={\\int }_{a}^{b}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nAlong the curve $C,$ we have\n\n$$\nx=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,y=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t.\n$$\n\nSo,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(t)) & =y\\,i-x\\,j \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\,j \\\\  & =-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) & =(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j)\\cdot (-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j) \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\cdot (-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)+(-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)\\cdot 2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\  & =-4{sin}^{2}⁡t-4{cos}^{2}⁡t \\\\  & =-4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Γ={∮}_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }(-4)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-4t\\,{|}_{0}^{2\\pi } \\\\  & =-4(2\\pi )-0 \\\\  & =-8\\pi \\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186415315 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179741 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "976e05d4-59a8-5a93-be2f-b22357f388b9" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Circulation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "09f4c3d7-9a04-5668-b2e0-7bc01b46d0de" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-25\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415282} {:answer/id #uuid "50325077-b1b2-55cc-a916-71cd3fe9281a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415288} {:answer/id #uuid "a6f96501-879d-531b-beaa-b191c153f318" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415297} {:answer/id #uuid "afd3784e-7e64-5098-b432-0a060374a546" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "25\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415298} {:answer/id #uuid "ffb55bf7-4c03-598b-b1a4-e6045754359b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-10\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415306}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "50325077-b1b2-55cc-a916-71cd3fe9281a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415288} :answer-field/id #uuid "1e78bf84-51cb-53d9-8508-33548a10f308" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415308}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "b79e3fb3-3865-5932-ba2e-8b5992286bc0" :question/math-academy-id "q-125481" :question/problem "Calculate the circulation $Γ$ of the vector field $F(x,y)=-y\\,i+x\\,j$ along the circle $C$ with equation ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=5$ oriented counterclockwise." :question/worked-solution "The closed curve $C$ is the circle of radius $\\sqrt{5}$ centered at the origin. It can be parameterized in the counterclockwise direction as\n\n$$\nr(t)=\\sqrt{5}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i+\\sqrt{5}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,j,\\,0\\le t\\le 2\\pi .\n$$\n\nThe circulation $Γ$ measures the total work done by a force $F$ in moving a particle along a *closed* curve $C.$\nIt is given by the formula\n\n$$\nΓ={∮}_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r={\\int }_{a}^{b}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nAlong the curve $C,$ we have\n\n$$\nx=\\sqrt{5}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,y=\\sqrt{5}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t.\n$$\n\nSo,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(t)) & =-y\\,i+x\\,j \\\\  & =-\\sqrt{5}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+\\sqrt{5}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\sqrt{5}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\sqrt{5}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\,j \\\\  & =-\\sqrt{5}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+\\sqrt{5}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) & =(-\\sqrt{5}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+\\sqrt{5}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j)\\cdot (-\\sqrt{5}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+\\sqrt{5}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j) \\\\  & =-\\sqrt{5}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\cdot (-\\sqrt{5}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)+\\sqrt{5}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\cdot \\sqrt{5}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\  & =5{sin}^{2}⁡t+5{cos}^{2}⁡t \\\\  & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Γ={∮}_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }5\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =5t\\,{|}_{0}^{2\\pi } \\\\  & =5(2\\pi )-0 \\\\  & =10\\pi \\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186415312 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179740 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "6f30993e-b5c7-5d8a-9b27-2aed2c9223e1" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Sign of a Circulation From a Diagram"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "11d3f253-290b-54bb-babf-a6a6ff8978c0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415283} {:answer/id #uuid "587cc8ba-51a2-5f70-b7ab-e29504848366" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415289} {:answer/id #uuid "a2f13577-89cf-5b55-b7e4-ab887172e61d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415296} {:answer/id #uuid "dad516a1-d6d5-51c7-9929-12667a17dd33" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415300} {:answer/id #uuid "fe81a0b5-f0a2-514d-9d40-4d11839b169e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415305}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "11d3f253-290b-54bb-babf-a6a6ff8978c0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415283} :answer-field/id #uuid "01518b07-f415-5bbf-8b57-bf0d859e6135" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415307}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "2e8f010b-1d85-5f55-981d-5803f1cc03c7" :question/math-academy-id "q-125645" :question/problem "![](images/98/981d966645eec322afbfe28a5a1f519eeab8b3557692dcce368a87fe748dddd6.png)\n\nConsider the ellipse $E$ and the vector field $F,$ shown above. Let $C$ be any path where $E$ is traversed once in the **clockwise** direction. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- ${∮}_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r>0$\n- ${∮}_{-C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r<0$\n- ${∮}_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r<0$" :question/worked-solution "The circulation $Γ$ measures the total work done by a force $F$ in moving a particle along a *closed* curve $C.$\nIt is given by the formula\n\n$$\nΓ={∮}_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r={\\int }_{a}^{b}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nWe now recall the following:\n\n- If the force works *with* the motion of the particle (i.e., the force helps to push the particle along the path), then ${∮}_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r>0.$\n\n- If the force works *against* the motion of the particle (i.e., the force impedes the motion of the particle as it moves along the path), then ${∮}_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r<0.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each statement.\n\n- Statement I is true, while statement III is false. It's clear from the picture below that $F$ assists the motion of the particle as it moves clockwise along $C.$ \n\n![](images/b2/b2473eb0c505d75d2c448c1a0d714d05d42737ce8d601401e925834a25ae9e14.png)\n\nSo, $Γ={∮}_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r>0.$\n\n- Statement II is true. It's clear from the picture below that $F$ works against the motion of the particle as it moves counterclockwise along $C.$\n\n![](images/85/8513d9d15d204b6f63f310a37465d6b8dfb21239ca412bac7d3b9936f2bf5ebf.png)\n\nSo, $Γ={∮}_{-C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r<0.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186415314 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179743 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "1da220a9-2afc-5b63-a423-a4d2b3de9567" :knowledge-point/title "Circulation in Conservative Vector Fields"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1e6bbb37-e5ee-59d8-9780-e4f18b4fd703" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of the statements are true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415286} {:answer/id #uuid "3daf5c5a-bb1b-59ca-aa2f-2855f76bd340" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415287} {:answer/id #uuid "5d659d54-987d-5ce7-9597-87369b068528" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415290} {:answer/id #uuid "68eb45d8-b286-53dc-b61c-d25ed72ae9e9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415293} {:answer/id #uuid "e36e6492-906e-584d-9d76-b86aee6f54cd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415301}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "68eb45d8-b286-53dc-b61c-d25ed72ae9e9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415293} :answer-field/id #uuid "340ffb7b-df1a-5614-b77d-8024725af8e0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415309}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "77e87c43-f6db-5082-9277-93ef32960dff" :question/math-academy-id "q-125659" :question/problem "Consider the vector field $F(x,y)=2x\\operatorname{arctan}⁡2y\\,i+\\frac{2{x}^{2}}{1+4{y}^{2}}\\,j.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $F$ is **not** conservative\n- ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=0$ for any smooth path $C\\in {R}^{2}$\n- ${∮}_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=0$ for any ellipse $C\\in {R}^{2}$" :question/worked-solution "If $F$ is a conservative vector field on ${R}^{2},$ then the circulation $Γ=0$ for any *closed* path $C\\in {R}^{2},$ that is \n\n$$\nΓ={∮}_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=0.\n$$\n\nWe notice that $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j$ is a vector field with domain $D={R}^{2}.$ Computing the partial derivatives, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=\\frac{4x}{1+4{y}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each statement in turn:\n\n- Statement I is false. The vector field $F$ is conservative since the partial derivatives are equal and continuous everywhere on ${R}^{2}.$\n\n- Statement II is false, while statement III is true. The result $Γ=0$ applies to closed curves only.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259842 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179743 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "1da220a9-2afc-5b63-a423-a4d2b3de9567" :knowledge-point/title "Circulation in Conservative Vector Fields"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1d38863e-8c06-597a-afb7-14092ea52376" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of the statements are true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257025} {:answer/id #uuid "302f71d0-ddcf-5469-b62a-8627f2f507ef" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257207} {:answer/id #uuid "49295a90-64c7-5cf1-b3f6-9c51f6fc366a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257421} {:answer/id #uuid "567c01fa-62a0-5797-ace6-12ba30bdbc9d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257526} {:answer/id #uuid "6ff9a7ed-4b4d-561a-afaa-30bbfcb2f18d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257764}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "302f71d0-ddcf-5469-b62a-8627f2f507ef" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257207} :answer-field/id #uuid "a4623d71-f288-52db-9325-b4d7a1224d2c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259325}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "2353653c-eb1d-5d31-b3c0-eed1015199de" :question/math-academy-id "q-125664" :question/problem "Consider the vector field $F(x,y)=({y}^{2}+3x)\\,i+(2xy+3)\\,j.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $F$ is conservative\n- ${∮}_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=0$ for any circle $C\\in {R}^{2}$\n- ${∮}_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=0$ for any closed path $C\\in {R}^{2}$" :question/worked-solution "If $F$ is a conservative vector field on ${R}^{2},$ then the circulation $Γ=0$ for any *closed* path $C\\in {R}^{2},$ that is \n\n$$\nΓ={∮}_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=0.\n$$\n\nWe notice that $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j$ is a vector field with domain $D={R}^{2}.$ Computing the partial derivatives, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=2y.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each statement in turn:\n\n- Statement I is true. The vector field $F$ is conservative since the partial derivatives are equal and continuous everywhere on ${R}^{2}.$\n\n- Statement II and III are true. The circulation $Γ=0$ for any closed path $C\\in {R}^{2}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\""}]}
